/* search-index.js — prebuilt full-text index (generated; excludes flashcards & exam questions). */
window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Key Terms & Learning Outcomes","secs":[{"s":"Key Terms & Learning Outcomes","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","A ★ marks something stated outright as tested."]},{"s":"Introduction to PMH Nursing","a":"introduction-to-pmh-nursing","k":["Introduction to PMH Nursing","Key Terms","Mental health (WHO) — Well-being in which the individual can realize their own potential, cope with the normal stresses of life, work productively, and contribute to the community.","Mental illness (APA) — Health conditions involving changes in emotion, thinking, or behavior, associated with distress and/or problems functioning in social, work, or family activities.","Diathesis-stress model — The most accepted explanation for mental illness. Diathesis = a biological predisposition; stress = any emotional or environmental stress or trauma. Many carry the predisposition; it becomes illness only when a cert…","Resilience — The ability and capacity to secure the resources needed to support personal well-being: optimism, competence, and a sense of mastery over obstacles. Determines the incidence, severity, and prognosis of mental illness.","Recovery — A process of change through which an individual improves their health and wellness, lives a self-directed life, and reaches their full potential. Possible even where cure is not.","Comorbidity — Two co-occurring chronic illnesses: two mental health disorders (depression and anxiety), or a mental and a medical one (depression and diabetes).","Dual diagnosis — A co-occurring mental illness and substance use disorder. One of the two must be a substance use disorder — that is what separates it from comorbidity.","Adherence (not compliance) — Following the plan of care, and the term that replaced compliance. \"Compliance\" carries a strongly negative connotation, so the language change is itself a way of breaking down stigma.","Deinstitutionalization — The mass movement of severely mentally ill people from state hospitals to outpatient care, closing many hospitals. Accelerated by Kennedy's 1963 Community Mental Health Act.","Revolving-door treatment — Repeated ED visits and inpatient admissions. Began in the 1960s: psychotropic drug development plus deinstitutionalization into communities that lacked services, leaving patients without the skills to function ind…","Mental Health Parity — Insurers must give equal coverage for psychiatric illness as for physical health — deductibles, number of covered visits, and out-of-pocket expense. Also covers substance-related disorders (1996).","Acute care hospitalization — Highly structured, 24-hour locked setting that optimizes safety and addresses crisis intervention. Average stay 3–7 days, and about 3–5 days on an adult unit.","Long-term hospitalization — Where the client is referred if they need more than 7 days to stabilize.","Partial hospitalization (PHP) — Transitional step between inpatient and fully independent home life, for people needing some supervision but not hospitalization. About 6 hours of treatment, multiple days a week.","Day treatment — One step below PHP: structured activities and group for ongoing, usually chronic mental illness, so family members can work. 2–3 times a week for about a month.","Learning Outcomes","The continuum: well-being → emotional problems or concerns → mental illness. People move along it in both directions.","What contributes to mental health: biologic, psychological, and sociocultural influences. Characteristics include rational thinking, resiliency, self-esteem, self-awareness, emotional growth, and self-care.","Stigma: seeing someone in a negative way because of a characteristic, attribute, or personal trait. It worsens the course and outcomes of mental illness.","Counter stigma by talking openly about mental health, educating yourself and others, using person-first language, decriminalizing, pushing back on media portrayals, and advocating for reform.","Barriers to care: too few providers, transportation, two-to-three-month waits for outpatient follow-up, low income and cost, the limits of insurance parity, and stigma itself.","Admission types: voluntary — the client signs themselves in and consents. Involuntary — admitted against their will on a 72-hour hold, not counting weekends and holidays. Form 202A is filed by the APRN or MD for longer commitment.","Admission criteria for an acute unit: danger to self, danger to others, or unable to care for basic needs and/or gross impairment of judgment.","Least restrictive means, in order: assess → verbally intervene → reduce stimulation → provide diversion → PRN by mouth → PRN IM → escort to seclusion → restraints last.","Chemical restraint is less restrictive than physical or mechanical restraint, and PO is always offered before IM.","The therapeutic milieu — five components: containment, support, validation, structure, involvement.","Consumers is the term for individuals who have mental illness; the consumer movement demanded involvement in their own treatment decisions. NAMI (1979) is the largest grassroots organization, and advocates person-first language: not \"a schi…","Patient rights: to have or refuse visitors, to receive or refuse treatment, and rights regarding psychiatric advance directives. Informed consent means risks, benefits, and alternatives were given and treatment voluntarily accepted.","Capacity versus competency: capacity is the ability to make an informed decision; competency is the legal term for the mental soundness required to decide or act.","Confidentiality breaks in exactly two circumstances: a specific threat to kill someone (duty to warn and protect), and suspected child or elder abuse.","The five components of the therapeutic milieu — \"Contain Some Very Stubborn Individuals\": Containment, Support, Validation, Structure, Involvement."]},{"s":"Neurobiology & Pharmacology","a":"neurobiology-pharmacology","k":["Neurobiology & Pharmacology","Key Terms","★ The eight neurotransmitters — Inhibitory: serotonin, GABA. Excitatory: dopamine, glutamate, epinephrine, norepinephrine. Other: acetylcholine, histamine.","Amygdala — The emotional center of the brain: emotion, anxiety, fear, and pleasure. Part of the fight-or-flight system, alerting us to danger.","★ Neuroplasticity — The ability of the brain to change with learning by reorganizing neural pathways. Why repetition of a skill or a behavior change matters.","Synaptic pruning — The process that removes unnecessary or damaged neuronal structures, weeding out weaker, seldom-used synapses. Surges prenatally through age two, in the preteen years, and at midlife.","Discontinuation syndrome — A sudden decrease or stop of certain psychotropics. The original symptoms rebound, and new physical or psychological symptoms appear. Antidepressants are not addicting, but the body does get used to them.","Psychopharmacology — Start low and titrate up slowly · start only one new medication at a time · use the lowest effective dose · weigh risks against benefits · hold or switch for significant side effects · taper, never stop abruptly.","The eight neurotransmitters — \"Silent Guards Inhibit; Dangerous Gangs Excite Neighborhoods\": Serotonin and GABA inhibit; Dopamine, Glutamate, Epinephrine and Norepinephrine excite. The two left over are the \"other\" pair — acetylcholine and …","Learning Outcomes","★ Five structures: thalamus, hypothalamus, cerebellum, brainstem, amygdala.","Thalamus: the main relay station of the CNS. Hypothalamus: temperature, appetite, sleep, sex drive, heart rate, blood pressure.","Hippocampus: long- and short-term memory. Heavily damaged in chronic alcoholism, leading to Korsakoff syndrome and Wernicke's.","Frontal damage: perseveration, emotional lability, poor planning and problem-solving, Broca's aphasia. Temporal damage: Wernicke's aphasia, prosopagnosia, memory loss, persistent talking.","Parietal damage: problems with math, reading, and left-right discrimination; apraxia. Occipital damage: visual field cuts, visual hallucinations, movement agnosia.","Illusion versus hallucination: in an illusion something really is there but is misperceived; in a hallucination nothing is.","Serotonin is decreased in depressive and anxiety disorders; blocking its reuptake treats both very successfully.","GABA is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS. Increase GABA to decrease anxiety.","Dopamine in excess produces auditory hallucinations and plays a role in schizophrenia. Glutamate is decreased in psychosis — the opposite pattern from dopamine.","Excess glutamate over-stimulates the brain and causes seizures; excessive release leads to excitotoxicity and cell death.","Acetylcholine is decreased in Alzheimer's and sleep disorders, increased in depression.","Norepinephrine is decreased in depression, increased in mania, anxiety, and schizophrenia. Epinephrine works with it in fight-or-flight.","Imaging: structural (CT, MRI) shows anatomy; functional (PET, SPECT, fMRI) shows activity and physiological change.","Over-pruning is one biological theory of schizophrenia, timed to the pruning surge in late adolescence and young adulthood."]},{"s":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","a":"anger-aggression-violence","k":["Anger, Aggression & Violence","Key Terms","Anger — An emotional response to frustration of desires, a threat to one's needs, or a challenge — mild irritation to intense fury. A normal emotion, capable of being under personal control; a problem only when expressed aggressively.","Aggression — Verbal or physical attack intended to threaten or injure the victim's security or self-esteem. Virtually always designed to punish, but not always inappropriate — sometimes necessary for self-protection.","Violence — Intentional use of force that results in, or could result in, injury to another. Always an objectionable act — that is what separates it from aggression.","Restraint — Any manual method, physical or mechanical device, material, or equipment that immobilizes or reduces a patient's ability to move arms, legs, body, or head freely.","Seclusion — Involuntary confinement of a patient alone in a room they are physically prevented from leaving. Less restrictive than restraints and never punitive; only for violent or self-destructive behavior that jeopardizes immediate physi…","★ Critical incident debriefing — Immediate and mandatory after a restraint episode, with patient and staff.","Learning Outcomes","Two predictors: hyperactivity is the most important predictor of imminent violence; a history of violence is the single best predictor of future violence.","Precipitants: anxiety (catch it early), withdrawal and substance use, sleep deprivation, pain, feeling out of control, and poor communication.","Warning signs: pacing and restlessness, clenched jaws or fists, changes in speech rate or volume, verbal abuse, glaring or wholly avoided eye contact, and uncharacteristic withdrawal.","Aggression occurs most often in the context of limit setting.","The four levels: anxiety → loss of rationality (defensive) → hostility, abuse or aggression → tension reduction, the reachable and teachable moment.","De-escalation: speak slowly in shortened sentences with a low, calm voice; offer two options to reduce powerlessness; give feedback on what you see; never yell.","Nurse safety: stay about one foot further than the patient can reach; keep an escape route and never let the patient between you and the door; no dangling jewellery; only one person talks.","Restraint hard rules: never prone; never a PRN or standing order; order and face-to-face assessment within one hour; two fingers under the restraint.","What silently counts as a restraint: four side rails up (except seizure precautions), sheets tucked so tightly the patient cannot move, holding someone against their will.","Seclusion is tried before restraint. Both are a last resort, only when the patient is a danger to self or others and less restrictive interventions have been found ineffective.","A restrained patient is never left alone. Remove restraints and do range of motion on one limb at a time, and always place a wedge pillow under the head of anyone restrained in bed.","Six contraindications to seclusion and restraint: extremely unstable medical or psychiatric conditions, COPD, spinal injury, seizure disorders, pregnancy, delirium or dementia.","The six contraindications — \"Pregnant Patients Should Simply Delay Cuffs\": Pregnancy, Psychiatric or medical instability, Spinal injury, Seizure disorders, Delirium or dementia, COPD."]},{"s":"Assessment, Theories & Therapies","a":"assessment-theories-therapies","k":["Assessment, Theories & Therapies","Key Terms","★ The five behavioral therapies — Modeling = role play · operant conditioning = tokens and positive reinforcement · systematic desensitization = relaxation plus graded exposure · aversion therapy = negative stimulus · biofeedback = control …","Interpersonal therapy (IPT) — Targets relationships by improving functioning and communication patterns. Very short-term: 12 to 16 sessions.","Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) — Our thoughts cause our feelings and behaviors, not external things like people or situations. Change the way we think and we change the way we feel, and act better even if the situation does not change.","Dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) — The integration of opposites: cognitive and behavioral techniques combined with mindfulness. Developed to treat chronically suicidal individuals, many of whom had borderline personality disorder.","Rational emotive therapy (RET) — Explores irrational thinking and beliefs, teaching the client to delete the \"must,\" \"should,\" \"ought,\" and \"have to.\"","★ Cognitive distortions (\"stinkin' thinkin'\") — All-or-nothing thinking · overgeneralization · mental filter · discounting the positive · jumping to conclusions · magnification · emotional reasoning · should statements · labeling · personal…","The five behavioral therapies — \"My Old Son Ate Bugs\": Modeling, Operant conditioning, Systematic desensitization, Aversion therapy, Biofeedback.","The ten cognitive distortions, in three groups. Seeing wrong — \"All Or Mostly Dark\": All-or-nothing thinking, Overgeneralization, Mental filter, Discounting the positive. Predicting wrong — \"Jumping Magnifies Emotions\": Jumping to conclusio…","Learning Outcomes","Purpose of the PMH assessment: establish rapport, understand the current problem, review physical status, assess risk and safety, assess psychosocial status, identify mutual goals, formulate the plan of care.","Depressed mood obliges a suicide assessment. Ask directly: \"Are you depressed?\" and then \"Do you feel that you might hurt yourself?\"","Affect versus mood: affect is what you observe; mood is the emotional state in the person's own words.","Affect range, most to least expressive: full/broad, constricted, blunted, flat. Flat resembles the masking seen in Parkinson's.","Affect congruency: congruent when mood and affect match; incongruent when they do not — \"I'm doing great,\" said while crying.","Mood terms: euthymic (normal), dysthymic (chronically low), elated or euphoric, labile (up and down), cyclothymic (cycling).","Psychosocial interventions are non-pharmacological techniques addressing the societal, familial, and cultural aspects: education, positive self-talk, self-monitoring, limit setting, relaxation, spiritual support, and discharge planning.","The three IPT techniques: identification of emotion, expression of emotion, and dealing with emotional baggage carried from past relationships.","Aversion therapy example: disulfiram (Antabuse) in alcohol dependence — drinking on it causes nausea, dizziness, and flushing.","The ABCs of RET: Activating event, Beliefs, emotional Consequences. The therapy works on B, because changing beliefs changes the consequences.","DBT's four skill sets: emotional regulation, interpersonal effectiveness, distress tolerance, mindfulness.","DBT's four modes of treatment: group skills training, individual psychotherapy, telephone contact, and therapist consultation. It takes one to two years before the best results show."]},{"s":"Group & Physiologic Therapies","a":"group-physiologic-therapies","k":["Group & Physiologic Therapies","Key Terms","Group — Two or more people pursuing common goals or interests, benefiting by mutually giving and receiving feedback. Patient and family education counts as leading a group.","Phototherapy (light therapy) — A fluorescent light box with a strong UV filter, about 10,000 lux, set off to the side. 30 to 45 minutes a day, usually in the morning. First-line for major depressive disorder with a seasonal pattern.","Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) — The most effective treatment for depression, and non-invasive. Done under anesthesia with a muscle relaxant; two to three treatments a week, 6 to 12 in total.","Transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) — Non-invasive MRI-strength magnetic pulses to focal areas of the cerebral cortex. FDA approved for treatment-resistant depression; outpatient, 30 minutes, five days a week for four to six weeks.","Vagus nerve stimulation (VNS) — Invasive: a pulse generator in the chest wired around the left cervical vagus nerve, pulsing 30 seconds every 5 minutes. FDA approved for partial onset seizures and depression.","Deep brain stimulation (DBS) — The most invasive and very seldom used: electrodes implanted in the chest, threaded up the neck, with probes placed into the brain. A Parkinson's treatment now investigated in depression.","Herbal treatments — Not regulated by the FDA, and concentrations vary between brands. St. John's Wort for mild-to-moderate depression · ginseng for stress or fatigue · valerian root and chamomile · SAM-e.","Exercise — Alters serotonin, dopamine, and norepinephrine, and dampens the HPA axis. Less expensive than ECT, with fewer side effects than medication.","Learning Outcomes","★ Yalom's 11 therapeutic factors: instillation of hope, universality, imparting of information, altruism, corrective recapitulation, development of socializing techniques, imitative behavior, interpersonal learning, group cohesiveness, cath…","★ The seven physiological treatments: phototherapy, ECT, VNS, TMS, DBS, herbal treatments, exercise.","★ Task roles: initiator-contributor, information seeker, information giver, coordinator, orienteer, recorder. Building and maintenance roles: encourager, harmonizer, gatekeeper, group observer, follower. Individual roles are the non-product…","Group characteristics: size, defined purpose, degree of similarity among members, rules, boundaries, and content and process.","Who can lead what: an RN may lead activity, educational, task, and support groups. Group psychotherapy requires an advanced degree, because it aims at personality change.","Types of groups: medication or health education, dual diagnosis, symptom management, recreational activity, interpersonal skills. The therapeutic community meeting is the essential inpatient group.","Advantages of group therapy: engages several clients at once, members benefit from others' feedback and life experience, a safe setting to try new behaviors, and promotes belonging.","Disadvantages: time constraints, confidentiality cannot be guaranteed, and disruptive members — someone acutely psychotic, manic, or intoxicated does not benefit from group.","Leadership styles: autocratic (leader exerts control, little interaction), democratic (extensive interaction in problem-solving), laissez-faire (members set the direction).","Rule out bipolar disorder before starting light therapy — it can trigger mania or hypomania.","ECT: an electrical current lasting 1 second or less produces a brief seizure of 30 to 60 seconds. One extremity is left un-paralyzed so the convulsion can be observed.","ECT is contraindicated in brain tumors and subdural hematoma. The heart is heavily stressed at the onset of the seizure and for up to ten minutes after.","After ECT: wakes about 15 minutes later, confusion and disorientation for several hours, and retrograde amnesia for the events leading up to and including the treatment.","TMS's one contraindication is metal in the area of stimulation — brain stimulators, cochlear implants, medication pumps, bullet fragments. Also not used with a pacemaker or a history of seizures.","VNS side effects: voice alteration in nearly 60%, plus hoarseness, headache, neck pain, cough, paresthesia, and dyspnea, all decreasing with time."]},{"s":"Not Tested","a":"not-tested","k":["Not Tested","Explicitly Excluded","Prevalence and incidence statistics — unless a specific one is called out.","How to score the Brøset or the DASA, or what is on either tool. Know only that structured tools exist and are used.","The specific monitoring intervals for a patient in seclusion or restraint.","The history of psychiatric mental health nursing — Dorothea Dix, 1837, 1882, 1946, 1973."]}]},{"f":"week1-intro.html","t":"Introduction to Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing","secs":[{"s":"Introduction to Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1"]},{"s":"Defining Mental Health & Mental Illness","a":"defining-mental-health-mental-illness","k":["Defining Mental Health & Mental Illness","Health (WHO) — Complete physical, mental, and social well-being: not merely the absence of disease or infirmity. Includes satisfaction with, and enjoyment of, the self and the environment.","Mental health (WHO) — Well-being in which the individual can realize their own potential, cope with the normal stresses of life, work productively, and contribute to the community.","Mental illness (APA) — Health conditions involving changes in emotion, thinking, or behavior, associated with distress and/or problems functioning in social, work, or family activities.","DSM-5 — Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 5th edition: the official medical guidelines of the American Psychiatric Association for diagnosing psychiatric disorders. Criteria influenced by multiprofessional clinical fiel…","Characteristics of Mental Health","Rational thinking","Resiliency","Self-esteem","Self-awareness","Emotional growth","Self-care","Learning and productivity","Communication skills","Meaningful relationships","Spiritual satisfaction","What Shapes Mental Health","Prenatal, perinatal, and neonatal events — Interactions — Family stability","Physical health status — Intelligence quotient — Ethnicity","Nutrition — Self-concept — Housing","History of injuries — Skills — Child-rearing patterns","Neuroanatomy — Creativity — Economic level","Physiology — Emotional developmental level — Religion, values, and beliefs","The Mental Health–Illness Continuum","Health is fluid, whether mental or physical. Mental health and mental illness sit on a continuum, and people move along it in both directions.","Well-Being","Adequate to high functioning. Everyday stress and discomfort do not impact daily functioning.","Emotional Problems or Concerns","Mild to moderate distress, mild impairment in functioning: trouble with concentration and appetite; insomnia and other sleep issues.","Mental Illness","Marked distress, with altered thinking, mood, and behavior.","Theories of Causation","Early 19th-Century Theories","Inheritance theory: \"insanity\" transmitted from one generation to another.","Moral degeneracy theory: mentally ill by virtue of a bad character.","Germ theory: \"insanity\" believed contagious, so people were segregated.","Septic foci theory: infection causes insanity, so remove the infection by surgery = cure. The era of bleeding and leeches.","What Is Believed Now","No single common cause: different mental illnesses have different theories of causation.","Diathesis-stress model: the most accepted explanation for mental illness. Diathesis = a biological predisposition for an illness; stress = any emotional or environmental stress or trauma. Many carry the predisposition, but it does not turn …"]},{"s":"History: Early Treatment & the Shift to Community Care","a":"history-early-treatment-the-shift-to-community-care","k":["History: Early Treatment & the Shift to Community Care","1800s–1900s: symptoms believed to be behavioral, under the person's control. Treatment followed from that belief.","Early Treatments","Physical restraint: padded helmets, hand mittens, chain restraints, straitjackets.","Chemical restraint: heavy medication that left people sitting around.","Lobotomy: stopped acting-out behaviors but drastically changed personality.","Insulin therapy and hydrotherapy (sedative cold wet packs, warm baths, and tanks that sprayed water at the person as a calming measure).","ECT, introduced later.","ECT is taught as a current treatment — full detail in Group & Physiologic Therapies","1845 — Lunacy Act: created asylums and institutionalization. Families dropped people off and often never saw them again.","1930 — Mental Health Treatment Act: institutions became Mental Hospitals; people began to be treated more humanely.","1963 — President John F. Kennedy's Community Mental Health Act accelerated deinstitutionalization: the mass movement of severely mentally ill people from state hospitals to outpatient care, closing many hospitals. Expanded the nurse's role:…","1970s — Mental hospitals shutting down; mental health units opened inside general hospitals, making mental health care more like acute medical care.","1990s — Strong push for less time in the hospital and more management in the community.","Late 2000s — Integration of primary care and mental health care in outpatient settings.","What Went Wrong After Deinstitutionalization","People had been institutionalized: no coping skills, no jobs, nowhere to live, no ability to cook or care for themselves.","Many with good family support or assisted living transitioned successfully, especially with the new medications; many others did not.","Community mental health centers grew slowly and were understaffed: no job training, psychiatric counseling, or life skills training.","Incarceration — About 2 million people with mental illness are booked into jails each year. Most get no treatment, so the illness worsens and victimization risk is high. Once out, at least 83% no longer have access to health care and benefi…","Revolving-door treatment — Began in the 1960s with the community mental health movement: psychotropic drug development plus deinstitutionalization into communities that lacked services, leaving patients without the skills to function indepe…","Managed care — 1990s: inpatient a few weeks to stabilize. For the past two decades managed care companies have dictated the terms of admission: length-of-stay reductions and more stringent admission criteria.","Victimization — People assume mental illness means dangerous, but people with mental illness are far more often the victims. Serious mental illness (SMI) carries a 12 times higher rate of victimization than the general population: verbal ab…"]},{"s":"Advocacy, Legislation & the Courts","a":"advocacy-legislation-the-courts","k":["Advocacy, Legislation & the Courts","Consumers — The term for individuals who have mental illness. The consumer (or consumer recovery) movement advocated for their rights and fought discrimination and forced treatment; consumers resisted treatment arranged for them by provider…","NAMI (1979) — National Alliance on Mental Illness: the nation's largest grassroots mental health organization.","What NAMI Does","Builds better services and treatment; supports research.","Communicates that mental illnesses are brain disorders: raising awareness and eliminating stigma and discrimination.","Advocates for people with mental illness and improves access to treatment services.","Integrates mental illness into community life.","Advocates for the language we use: the person is not the disease. Not \"a schizophrenic\"; a person who has schizophrenia.","Landmark Reports and Legislation","Mental Health Parity Act — 1996 — Insurers must give equal coverage for psychiatric illness as for physical health, including deductibles, number of covered visits, and out-of-pocket expense. Also covers substance-related disorders.","Report of the Surgeon General — 1999 — First national report on mental health in the US. Finding: mental health is fundamental to a healthy life · mental health disorders are real health conditions with enormous consequences · effective tre…","New Freedom Commission on Mental Health — 2003 — Finding: mental health care in the US was \"in shambles.\" Called for a streamlined system with less fragmented care, early diagnosis and treatment, adoption of the principles of recovery, and …","Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act — 2010 — Coverage for most uninsured Americans through expanded Medicaid eligibility; created health insurance exchanges for more choices; carried the insurance mandate.","Tim's Law (Kentucky) — 2017 — Kentucky's first assisted outpatient treatment (AOT) statute; other states call this assertive community treatment (ACT). Lets families or others in the community go to court to require an individual to attend …","The Six Goals of the New Freedom Commission","Americans understand the link between mental health and overall health.","Mental health care is family- and consumer-driven.","Disparities in mental health care are eliminated.","Screening, assessment, and referral become common practice, so problems are caught early.","Principles of recovery guide care.","Greater use of technology, and more housing for individuals with mental illness.","Fayette County Mental Health Court","October 2012: hosted by NAMI, attended by mental health and criminal justice stakeholders.","Multidisciplinary team: judges, prosecutors, providers, case workers, and peer support specialists (consumers), delivering treatment-oriented, person-centered care.","Built on therapeutic jurisprudence and restorative justice principles, to reduce re-offending.","Peer support specialists act as \"systems navigators,\" matching participants to housing and support services; independent housing with access to community services is a primary goal.","Referral by order of diversion, probation, or contempt. Staff then screen candidates for eligibility: verifying a diagnosis of serious mental illness and evaluating which services the person can use. The candidate decides whether to accept …","Lets people with SMI obtain professional treatment and medications instead of a jail sentence."]},{"s":"Barriers to Care & Stigma","a":"barriers-to-care-stigma","k":["Barriers to Care & Stigma","Access and provider limitations — Not as many providers, and limits on their current knowledge.","Transportation — A real barrier for many individuals.","Wait times — Two to three months for an outpatient follow-up appointment is common.","Income, cost, and insurance parity — Low income, the cost of care, and the limits of what insurance parity actually covers.","Stigma — Seeing someone in a negative way because of a particular characteristic, attribute, or personal trait (Mayo Clinic). Widespread fear and misunderstanding of mental illness, heightened by the focus on extraordinary symptoms in film …","Why Stigma Matters Clinically","A major clinical and public health issue in its own right.","Worsens the course and outcomes of mental illness; a heavy burden on the individual.","Many people with mental illness will not go to an ER because of stigma from health care providers.","How to Fight Stigma","Talk openly about mental health: break the cultural barrier of not talking about it.","Stop criminalizing these individuals (Mental Health Court is one way).","Push back on media portrayals of mental illness.","Educate yourself and others: education decreases stigma.","Be conscious of the language you use.","Advocate for mental health reform as a nurse.","Encourage equality in physical and mental illness.","See the person, not the illness; show empathy and compassion."]},{"s":"Key Terms","a":"key-terms","k":["Key Terms","Resilience — The ability and capacity to secure the resources needed to support personal well-being: optimism, competence, and a sense of mastery over obstacles. It determines the incidence, severity, and prognosis of mental illness and is …","Recovery — A process of change through which an individual improves their health and wellness, lives a self-directed life, and reaches their full potential. Recovery is possible even where cure is not: we cannot cure schizophrenia, and a pe…","Dual diagnosis — A co-occurring mental illness and a substance use disorder (e.g. alcohol use disorder with bipolar disorder). One of the two must be a substance use disorder.","Comorbidity — The broader term for two co-occurring chronic illnesses: two mental health disorders (depression and anxiety) or a mental and a medical one (depression and diabetes). Dual diagnosis is the specific case that involves substance…","Adherence (not compliance) — Following the plan of care, and the term that replaced compliance; \"compliance\" carries a strongly negative connotation, so the language change is itself a way of breaking down stigma.","Anosognosia — An individual with a severe mental illness does not believe they have it: a common reason a stabilized patient stops their medications at discharge."]},{"s":"Psychiatric Nursing Practice & the Treatment Team","a":"psychiatric-nursing-practice-the-treatment-team","k":["Psychiatric Nursing Practice & the Treatment Team","Basic Level: PMH-RN","Psychiatric mental health registered nurse.","Requires 2 years of full-time work, 2,000 clinical hours, 30 hours of continuing education.","Then sit the certification exam and add BC to the RN title (RN-BC).","Advanced Practice: PMH-APRN","Master of Science or doctorate.","Health promotion, screening, and evaluation; health assessment and examination; formulates differential diagnoses.","Orders, conducts, and interprets laboratory and diagnostic studies and procedures.","Conducts individual, family, and group psychotherapy: outside the basic-level scope.","Prescribes, monitors, manages, and evaluates medications.","Provides or directs home health services; also works in general health settings.","Nursing Skills Rooted in Psychiatric Mental Health Care","Safety: awareness of the client's environment and the client's thoughts.","Therapeutic communication and the nurse-patient relationship.","Milieu management.","Relaxation therapy.","Motivational interviewing and the concept of self-efficacy.","Suicide assessment and prevention.","Management of aggressive behavior, including with medication.","Crisis intervention and disaster mental health.","Instillation of hope: without hope, clients do not do well.","Instillation of hope is also one of Yalom's therapeutic factors — see Group & Physiologic Therapies","The Interdisciplinary Treatment Team","Each discipline gathers data and participates in the plan of care. The treatment plan (some places call it a clinical pathway) guides care during the stay, detailing the steps that deliver evidence-based care. Nurses frequently lead these m…","Who Is on the Team","Always: the patient, family, nurse, and provider.","May also include: CNS or APRN, psychologist, social worker (SW/LCSW), pharmacist, recreational (activity) therapist, and mental health associates or techs.","Certified drug and alcohol counselor when there is a substance use diagnosis; a Fayette County teacher on the child and adolescent units.","The patient is the most important member: they have self-efficacy and need to help design the plan. If they don't agree with it, they won't follow it.","Treatment Settings","Acute care hospital unit — Highly structured, 24-hour locked setting that optimizes safety and addresses crisis intervention. Get them in, get them stable, get them somewhere else. — Average stay 3–7 days, and about 3–5 days on an adult uni…","Residential treatment for substance use — The person lives there and focuses on recovery. — Usually 30–60 days; can run 6–12 months.","Partial hospitalization (PHP) — Transitional step between inpatient and fully independent home life, for people needing some supervision but not hospitalization: group therapy, occupational or therapeutic activities, recreation, then home. …","Day treatment — One step below PHP. Structured activities and group for ongoing, usually chronic mental illness (e.g. severe autism spectrum disorder), so family members can work. Focus: coping skills, strengths for a more functional, indep…","Specialty treatment settings — Pediatric psychiatric care · geriatric psychiatric care and geriatric units · veteran centers · forensic psychiatric care (clients in jails) · alcohol and drug use disorder treatment · self-help options · eati…","What an Acute Unit Is Actually Like","Admission criteria: danger to self (suicidal ideation or behaviors) · danger to others (homicidal ideation) · unable to care for basic needs and/or gross impairment of judgment, placing the individual at imminent risk through an inability t…","Some units also handle detoxification from drugs or alcohol.","Top 5 inpatient diagnoses: mood disorders (depressive and bipolar) · substance-related disorders · delirium or dementia · anxiety disorders · schizophrenia."]},{"s":"Admissions, Patient Rights & Legal Issues","a":"admissions-patient-rights-legal-issues","k":["Admissions, Patient Rights & Legal Issues","Voluntary Admission","The patient or guardian seeks hospitalization.","Both the individual and the health care professional must agree treatment is needed.","The client signs themselves in and signs a consent for treatment.","Involuntary Commitment","An emergency, very temporary admission typically involving people who are confused or psychotic.","The physician, PA, or NP believes admission is needed but the client does not want to be admitted, and is admitted against their will on a 72-hour hold, not counting weekends and holidays.","During the 72 hours the patient may improve and sign themselves in, becoming a voluntary admission.","If treatment is still needed when the 72 hours expire, form 202A is completed by the APRN or MD and taken to the judge to file for longer commitment.","Patients admitted to a psychiatric hospital have the same rights as any other hospitalized patient.","Rights guaranteed under the law — To have or refuse visitors, to receive or refuse treatment, and rights regarding psychiatric advance directives.","Informed consent — Patient informed of the risks, benefits, and alternatives, then voluntarily accepts treatment.","Implied consent — A patient is approached with a medication and indicates they are willing to take it.","Capacity — A person's ability to make an informed decision.","Competency — A legal term for the degree of mental soundness a person has to make decisions or to carry out specific acts.","Restraint and seclusion — There must be an order and documentation. In an emergency a nurse may place a patient in seclusion or restraint, then obtain a written or verbal order as soon as possible thereafter.","Confidentiality — Only those who need to know may know, unless the client gives permission. Two exceptions: a specific threat to kill someone (you have a duty to warn and protect third parties), and suspected child or elder abuse, which mus…","There is never a PRN or standing order for restraint. A physician must come, lay eyes on the patient, and write the order every time.","Least Restrictive Means","Assess what is going on — Find what is actually driving the behavior before intervening.","Verbally intervene — Talk to the client; use active listening.","Reduce stimulation — Encourage a client in the day room to go to their room; removing the client removes the audience, and things often calm down.","Provide diversion — Sometimes enough on its own to avoid restraint.","Offer PRN medication by mouth — Always offered before an injection; if refused, give the PRN IM (typically haloperidol, diphenhydramine, and lorazepam). Chemical restraints (medications ordered specifically for agitation) are less restricti…","Escort to seclusion, if agreed — Walk with them if they agree to go.","Apply restraints (last resort) — Only if the client is at significant danger to themselves or others.","Seclusion and restraint procedure, monitoring, and debriefing are taught in full in Anger, Aggression & Violence"]},{"s":"The Therapeutic Milieu","a":"the-therapeutic-milieu","k":["The Therapeutic Milieu","The therapeutic milieu is the surroundings and environment of the inpatient unit, including peer and staff interactions.","Containment — Basic needs (food, shelter, safety, security) are provided on a locked unit.","Support — Encouragement, praise, and positive feedback help clients achieve autonomy, improve coping skills, and manage symptoms.","Validation — Respecting privacy, cultural needs, and feelings for holistic health.","Structure — Control and limitation of maladaptive behaviors; setting limits.","Involvement — Promoting the self-efficacy of the client through self-care and stress management strategies.","Community meetings are the milieu's core group — covered in Group & Physiologic Therapies"]}]},{"f":"week1-neurobiology.html","t":"Neurobiology & Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Neurobiology & Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2"]},{"s":"Visualizing the Brain","a":"visualizing-the-brain","k":["Visualizing the Brain","Brain imaging began only in the 1990s, so the physiology of mental illness is still a relatively new field.","Structured Imaging","Shows anatomy; locates tumors, atrophy, and other abnormalities.","Computed tomography (CT)","Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)","Functional Imaging","Shows brain function and physiological activity: the changes that correlate with behavioral or cognitive alterations.","Positron emission tomography (PET)","Single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT)","Functional MRI (fMRI)","Brains with mental illness look different from brains without it. PET shows reduced brain activity (darker colors) in depression, especially in the prefrontal cortex. After treatment and recovery the entire brain, and particularly the left …"]},{"s":"Major Brain Functions","a":"major-brain-functions","k":["Major Brain Functions","Maintaining homeostasis — Keeping the internal environment stable despite the external one. The brain is the control center, regulating through the nervous and endocrine systems.","Negative feedback loops — A change is detected (low blood sugar) → the brain initiates a response (hunger) → the person eats → balance is restored.","Regulating the ANS and hormones — The autonomic nervous system runs involuntary functions: sympathetic = fight or flight, speeds body functions up; parasympathetic = rest and digest, slows them down. The endocrine system links the brain to …","Biological drives and behavior — Sex, hunger, the sleep-wakefulness cycle, and circadian rhythms.","Conscious mental activity — Thinking, memory, problem-solving, and interpretation of the external world.","Why a mental disturbance produces physical signs: anxiety activates the sympathetic nervous system, so the person doesn't just feel anxious; they get increased heart rate, increased blood pressure, sweating, and shortness of breath."]},{"s":"Core CNS Structures","a":"core-cns-structures","k":["Core CNS Structures","Thalamus — The main relay station of the CNS: sensory pathways from the spinal cord, cerebellum, and brainstem form synapses here on their way to the cerebral cortex.","Hypothalamus — The major respiratory center, holding the basic vital functions: temperature, appetite, sleep, sex drive, heart rate, and blood pressure.","Cerebellum — Motor coordination. The coiled structure under the occipital lobe, coordinating voluntary movement, equilibrium, and muscle tone.","Brainstem — The central core of the brain, made mostly of nerve fibers, in three areas: midbrain, pons, and medulla. Provides the main motor and sensory innervation to the face and neck through the cranial nerves; 10 of the 12 pairs come fr…"]},{"s":"The Cerebral Lobes & What Damage Looks Like","a":"the-cerebral-lobes-what-damage-looks-like","k":["The Cerebral Lobes & What Damage Looks Like","Frontal — Where thought processes occur. Executive function: distinguishing good from bad, judging consequences, goal-directed behavior; planning, organizing, control of emotions and behavior, and part of personality. — Inability to plan se…","Parietal — Perception, spelling, arithmetic, and reading: making sense of the world. — Problems with math, reading, drawing, and naming objects; difficulty distinguishing left from right; lack of awareness of certain body parts or surroundi…","Temporal — Receiving and identifying sensory information: memory, understanding, auditory, language. — Difficulty recognizing faces (prosopagnosia); difficulty understanding spoken words (Wernicke's aphasia); difficulty identifying and verb…","Occipital — The vision center. — Defects in vision (visual field cuts); hallucinations; inaccurately seeing objects (visual illusions); inability to recognize words and drawn objects; inability to recognize movement (movement agnosia).","Illusion vs. hallucination. An illusion: something really is there but is seen incorrectly, such as a coat tree across the room perceived as a person. A hallucination: nothing is there.","Where These Show Up Clinically","Frontal: perseveration plays a role in OCD; mood and lability in bipolar disorder.","Parietal: lack of awareness of body parts or surrounding space is seen a lot in schizophrenia and in nihilistic delusions. Plaque builds up here in Alzheimer's, and people lose the ability to draw.","Temporal: persistent talking is what you see in mania."]},{"s":"Emotion, Memory & Movement Structures","a":"emotion-memory-movement-structures","k":["Emotion, Memory & Movement Structures","Corpus callosum — Relays information between the two hemispheres. — Difficulty integrating logical information with emotion. Left (logical) side dominant: less ability to feel emotion. Right (emotional) side dominant: difficulty controlling…","Limbic system — Learning, memories, and emotions. Coordinates visceral responses: gut feelings, the hair rising on the back of your neck. — Excessive emotional responses.","Hippocampus — Both long- and short-term memory; also controls the endocrine and immune systems. — Cannot form new memories despite intact intelligence. Heavily damaged in chronic alcoholism, which leads to Korsakoff syndrome and Wernicke's.","Amygdala — The emotional center of the brain: emotion, anxiety, fear, and pleasure. Part of the fight-or-flight system: alerts us to danger with enough fear and anxiety to protect us. — Damage in animals can cause rage. Plays a huge role in…","Basal ganglia — Coordinates and assists in moving the skeletal muscles. — —","Putting It Together: How a Touch Is Processed","1. Peripheral nervous system — The touch signal travels to the sensory cortex.","2. Sensory cortex — Evaluates it: what does that feel like?","3. Hippocampus — Searches for a memory of being touched like this. Good or bad? What do I expect next?","4. Amygdala — Attaches the emotional connection of that earlier remembered touch.","5. Basal ganglia — Causes you to act: withdraw in fear, return the touch with similar gentleness, or strike back with similar force."]},{"s":"The Neurotransmitters","a":"the-neurotransmitters","k":["The Neurotransmitters","Neurotransmitter: a substance released when a presynaptic neuron is excited, acting by either inhibiting or exciting a target cell. Released from vesicles at the end of the axons across the synaptic cleft, to receptors on the other side.","Too much or too little of these neurotransmitters is believed to cause neurological and mental illness. Drugs act on them by enhancing release, inhibiting release, or blocking synthesis; many also block reuptake, so a higher concentration s…","There are hundreds of neurotransmitters; this course covers eight. Inhibitory: serotonin · GABA Excitatory: norepinephrine · epinephrine · dopamine · glutamate Other: acetylcholine · histamine","Serotonin — Inhibitory — Modulates mood, emotion, sleep, pain, and appetite as related to mood; temperature regulation; some cognitive function. — Decreased in depressive and anxiety disorders; blocking its reuptake treats both very success…","GABA(gamma-aminobutyric acid) — Inhibitory — The major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS. Helps neurons recover after transmission, reduces anxiety, aggression, and stress, and regulates norepinephrine, adrenaline, dopamine, and seroto…","Norepinephrine — Excitatory — Affects mood, attention, and arousal. Stimulates the sympathetic branch of the ANS for the fight-or-flight stress response. — Decreased in depression; increased in mania, anxiety, and schizophrenia.","Epinephrine(adrenaline) — Excitatory — Works with norepinephrine in fight-or-flight to boost strength, heart rate, blood pressure, and energy. Also decreases the ability to feel pain. — —","Dopamine — Excitatory — Pleasurable feelings and complex motor activity; fine muscle movement; integration of emotions and thoughts; decision-making. — Too much can lead to auditory hallucinations. Plays a role in schizophrenia, in both the…","Glutamate — Excitatory — Used at most fast excitatory synapses in the brain and spinal cord. Stimulates the hypothalamus to release thyroid, adrenal, and sex hormones. — Excess over-stimulates the brain and causes seizures; excessive releas…","Acetylcholine — Excitatory or inhibitory — Learning, memory, cognitive functioning; sleep-wake cycles. Regulates mood: mania and sexual aggression. — Decreased in Alzheimer's and in sleep disorders; increased in depression.","Histamine — Other — Listed among the eight; no functions covered."]},{"s":"Neuroplasticity & Synaptic Pruning","a":"neuroplasticity-synaptic-pruning","k":["Neuroplasticity & Synaptic Pruning","Neuroplasticity","The brain's lifelong ability to change with learning, reorganizing neural pathways based on new experiences.","As we learn and acquire knowledge and skills, new pathways are laid down; learning a fact or skill requires persistent functional changes in the brain.","This is why repetition of a skill or a behavior change matters.","Synaptic Pruning","The neurological process that removes unnecessary or damaged neuronal structures.","Weeds out the weaker, seldom-used synapses, improving the brain's \"networking\" capacity.","The simpler associations formed in childhood are thought to be replaced by learning and more complex structures.","Ongoing, but it speeds up in prenatal development through age two, the preteen years, and midlife.","The link to schizophrenia: one biological theory is over-pruning, which can result in hallucinations; the timing fits the pruning surge in late adolescence and young adulthood."]},{"s":"Psychopharmacology Principles","a":"psychopharmacology-principles","k":["Psychopharmacology Principles","Start low and titrate up slowly — Titrate to the patient's response; if the response is there, no increase may be needed.","Hold or switch for significant side effects — The medication may be held temporarily, or changed.","Start only one new medication at a time — So you can identify which medication caused a side effect or reaction.","Weigh the risks against the benefits — Especially in older adults and in polypharmacy (many prescribed medications).","Use the lowest effective dose — Enough for the benefit, with fewer side effects. Especially in older adults, who may be more sensitive, and in children.","Taper; never stop abruptly — Gradually reduce to prevent withdrawal, rebound, or worsening of symptoms.","What Changes How a Medication Behaves","Other medications: increase or decrease a drug's metabolism.","Diet: affects how medications are absorbed and metabolized, including half-life (the time the body takes to halve the drug's concentration). Matters most with a narrow therapeutic index or easy toxicity; lithium, where sodium and fluid inta…","Genetic differences: fast versus slow metabolizers, affecting both drug levels and side effects. Genetic variation differs across populations, so psychiatric prescribing has to be individualized.","Discontinuation Syndrome","Antidepressants are not addicting (people do not abuse them or crave them), but the body does get used to them. A sudden decrease in dosage, or a sudden stop of certain psychotropic medications, causes discontinuation syndrome. Teach patien…","Rebound or relapse — The original symptoms come back.","New symptoms appear — Physical or psychological symptoms that were not there before."]}]},{"f":"week1-anger-aggression-violence.html","t":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","secs":[{"s":"Anger, Aggression & Violence","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3"]},{"s":"Definitions","a":"definitions","k":["Definitions","Anger — An emotional response to frustration of desires, a threat to one's needs, or a challenge, from mild irritation to intense fury and rage. A normal human emotion, like anxiety, and capable of being under personal control: expressed as…","Aggression — Verbal or physical attack intended to threaten or injure the victim's security or self-esteem: words, fists, kicks, weapons. Virtually always designed to punish, but not always inappropriate: sometimes necessary for self-protec…","Violence — Intentional use of force that results in, or could result in, injury to another. Always an objectionable act; that is what separates it from aggression.","Workplace Violence","Why It Matters to Nurses","Health care and social services workers are at higher risk of workplace violence injury than workers overall.","Nurses are frequent targets because they have the most direct patient care contact.","Workplace violence is underreported, and it impairs your ability to provide effective patient care.","Most frequent settings: emergency departments · psychiatric units · geriatric units · intensive care units, but it can happen anywhere."]},{"s":"What Precipitates Anger & Violence","a":"what-precipitates-anger-violence","k":["What Precipitates Anger & Violence","Feelings That May Precipitate Anger","Anxiety — Discounted — Embarrassed — Frightened","Fear — Humiliated — Hurt — Ignored","Inadequate — Insecure — Unheard — Out of control of the situation","Rejected — Threatened — Tired — Vulnerable","Anxiety is the one to catch early. Anxious patients, visitors, and families grasp at anything in the environment they can control. Intervene early, and communicate; communication is how we give them back a little control.","Situations That May Precipitate Anger","Withdrawal and substance use — Very common; patients frequently carry a co-occurring substance use disorder alongside a psychiatric disorder (dual diagnosis).","Symptom burden — Not every patient with a psychiatric diagnosis becomes angry, aggressive, or violent, but auditory hallucinations and psychosis make it more likely.","Sleep deprivation — Huge factor: staff in and out of the room, symptoms keeping them awake.","Pain, extreme stress, trauma, attention-seeking behaviors — Each precipitates anger.","Feeling out of control — Loss of personal power.","Difference in expectations — Their goals differ from ours and they feel we are not meeting them.","Difference in knowledge — Heavy medical jargon, used as though the patient understands it.","Poor communication — A major contributor, along with inaccurate or incomplete information.","Milieu Characteristics Conducive to Violence","Too hot, too cold, too loud, too quiet","Overcrowding","Staff perceived as inattentive or inexperienced","Staff perceived as too controlling","Poor limit setting: the sense that other patients are allowed to do things they are not","Revocation of privileges","The therapeutic milieu and its five components are taught in Introduction to Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing"]},{"s":"Warning Signs of Potential Violence","a":"warning-signs-of-potential-violence","k":["Warning Signs of Potential Violence","Hyperactivity is the most important predictor of imminent violence: pacing, restlessness.","These signs usually (but not always) precede violence.","Motor — Hyperactivity, pacing, restlessness, fidgeting.","Tension — Clenched jaws or fists, rigid posture, fixed or tensed facial expressions; mumbling.","Speech — Changes in rate, rhythm, volume: a loud or very soft voice, change of pitch, or stone silence.","Verbal content — Verbal abuse, profanity, argumentativeness.","Eye contact — Intense eye contact or glaring, or avoiding it completely.","History and objects — Recent acts of violence; a weapon, or an object usable as one.","Withdrawal — Isolation that is uncharacteristic for that individual."]},{"s":"Assessing the Risk of Violence","a":"assessing-the-risk-of-violence","k":["Assessing the Risk of Violence","The best predictor of future behavior is past behavior. Use structured tools rather than unstructured clinical judgment alone, to monitor and reduce incidents and to build a risk management plan.","History of violence — The single best predictor of future violence, including any assaultive or threatening behavior. Reactions to stimuli come from previous experience, so identify past and current triggers.","Symptom burden — Delusions, hyperactivity, impulsivity, predisposition to irritability.","Cognitive changes — Confusion, disorientation, delirium, psychosis, acute hallucinations, delusions; they may misinterpret the environment or staff care activities.","Agitation and escalating anxiety — Restlessness, and especially escalation (mild to severe).","Non-adherence or resistance to treatment — Some do not believe they have a mental illness.","Known history of substance use — Raises risk on its own.","Aggression management needed at transfer — Required aggression management on arrival; they did not come to the unit willingly.","Limit setting by the nurse — Aggression occurs most often in the context of limit setting.","Limited coping or assertiveness — Limited coping skills; lack of assertiveness, or use of intimidation.","Risk Assessment Tools","BVC — Brøset Violence Checklist","DASA — Dynamic Appraisal of Situational Aggression","ABRAT — Aggressive Behaviour Risk Assessment Tool","MOAS — Modified Overt Aggression Scale","COVR — Classification of Violence Risk","HCR-20 — Historical Clinical Risk-20","START — Short-Term Assessment of Risk and Treatability","How to score a Brøset or DASA is not exam material. What matters is that structured tools exist and are used to identify patients at risk. The COVR is the one used to assess risk at discharge.","Brøset Violence Checklist (BVC)","Developed in Norway; the most common tool in the literature.","A shift assessment, scored on behaviors present versus the individual's own baseline.","Dynamic Appraisal of Situational Aggression (DASA)","Based on the Brøset. Used at Eastern State Hospital.","Completed every 24 hours rather than every 8-hour shift, covering seven areas.","More reliable than the other tools at predicting verbal and physical aggression.","UK HealthCare Patient Violence Risk Assessment (in EPIC)","When it is done — Every patient screened on admission (ED or floor), then, whatever their risk level, reassessed every 12 hours.","How it communicates — Populates to the demographic pane of the chart as an FYI flag, so it travels with the patient across the hospital. A sign is also placed on the door.","History of violence — Verbal or physical aggression at any point in the health care setting. Remains on the banner for the life of the EHR.","Potential for violence — Falls off the banner at discharge."]},{"s":"Interventions & Personal Safety","a":"interventions-personal-safety","k":["Interventions & Personal Safety","Self-Awareness","Nurses bring their own personal and professional history, and it affects the ability to intervene effectively and appropriately.","Be self-aware of your strengths, needs, concerns, and vulnerability.","Without self-awareness, nursing interventions become impulsive or emotion-based.","Watch your choice of words, tone of voice, nonverbal communication, and facial expressions.","Intervention ideally begins before any sign of escalation. Determining what the patient is feeling is already intervening, and often it is just saying you are concerned and want to listen.","Interventions","Acknowledge that the patient has needs, rational or not, possible to meet or not.","State your expectations clearly, usually through limit setting.","Have numerous brief, non-threatening, non-directive interactions to build the therapeutic relationship.","Speak slowly, in shortened sentences, with a low, calm voice: as anger escalates, the ability to process decreases drastically.","Never yell. Model controlled behavior: you are the reference point for the behavior you want.","Use open-ended statements: \"You feel people are treating you unfairly?\" not the challenging, \"What is going on right now?\"","Avoid asking \"Okay?\": it implies choices exist where they don't.","Offer two options: \"Your room, or the quiet room?\" This decreases the sense of powerlessness that often precipitates violence and moves the choice to the patient.","Give feedback on what you see: \"You seem to be very upset\" opens the feelings up and may de-escalate.","Personal space and escape route. Patients at risk for violence need more personal space: stay about one foot further than they can reach with arms or legs, so you read as less of a threat. Always leave yourself an escape route, and never le…","Staff Safety Considerations","Maintain eye contact but don't glare.","No dangling earrings, necklaces, or scarves; a patient may grab them and cause serious injury.","Enough staff for backup, but only one person talks; the rest stay unobtrusive.","Know the layout: furniture, obstacles, hazards that could injure during a physical intervention.","Don't stand directly in front of the patient or the doorway; stand to the side or at an angle, and encourage the patient to sit.","Talk somewhere quiet but visible to other staff, and tell staff you are with the patient.","Individualized Interventions: Coping Skills","Patients arrive with their own coping skills, and the hospital takes many away: no smoking, no alcohol, a restricted diet, no caffeine.","Assess current coping strategies — What do they use when upset, anxious, stressed, or angry? Which have not worked?","Substitute what is unavailable — A smoker gets a nicotine patch; a gamer gets a TV show; a favorite food gets an alternative.","Help them adopt new strategies — Skills they can use here and afterward.","Pharmacological Interventions","It is the nurse's role to assess the appropriateness of PRN medication. A PRN is sometimes helpful, even essential, as a patient escalates. Used in conjunction with psychosocial interventions and de-escalation, never instead of them.","Long-term treatment of anger, aggression, and violence is treatment of the underlying psychiatric disorder. The PRN handles the moment; it is not the plan."]},{"s":"Matching the Response to the Behavior","a":"matching-the-response-to-the-behavior","k":["Matching the Response to the Behavior","Intervene early, and make the staff response fit the level the patient is at. Laying hands on a patient who is merely anxious will escalate the situation.","Level 1: Anxiety","Patient Behavior","Anxiety, frustration, impatience, irritability; highly animated, repetitive movements.","A change in behavior in either direction: calm to loud, or loud to calm.","The patient still has some level of rationality.","Staff Response: Offer Support","Stay alert to changes in anxiety, anger, frustration, and nonverbal cues.","Approach as soon as you suspect a problem; open a dialogue and get them talking.","Acknowledge their feelings and needs. It is OK to guess wrong; trying to understand is the point.","Stay calm and in control; calm, even tone.","Reflect their emotion; be personable, a real human being, not cold or uncaring.","Avoid institutional or systems jargon.","Don't take the bait: don't get defensive, counterattack, or be easily offended.","Level 2: Loss of Rationality (Defensive)","Challenging questions: \"What are you going to do if I don't go to my room?\"","Standing in your personal space; intimidation tactics (staring, glaring).","Refusing: loudly and adamantly refusing requests, care, or policy.","Releasing: table pounding, loud sighing, throwing things but not at you, not meant to cause harm.","Staff Response","Isolate the interaction: move the patient, or move others away.","Reduce stimulation; remove people staring, laughing, or talking loudly.","Speak clearly and slowly, use few words, and show non-verbally what you want them to do.","Be firm but empathetic.","Allow loud verbal expressions of anger as long as they are not threatening.","Watch your paraverbals (tone, volume, and rhythm of speech): how you say what you say.","It is not a win-lose situation.","Again: keep an exit, and never place the patient between you and it.","Level 3: Hostility, Abuse, or Aggression","May begin as accusations, comments about your competence, or irrelevant personal remarks.","Danger signs: persistent swearing; sexist or racist comments; personal or specific threats of harm; intimidating comments; terroristic-type threats; any physical behavior directed at a person.","Call for help: coworkers, security, or law enforcement.","Protect yourself. Remove yourself from the situation if you can.","Remove objects within reach that could be used as weapons.","Never let the patient be between you and the exit.","Watch your nonverbals: don't sigh, roll your eyes, or show frustration; these escalate.","Keep directions to the patient short (\"please stay seated,\" \"stop, please\").","Don't agree just to agree.","If they can hear you, explain that aggression will not achieve the desired goal.","Level 4: Tension Reduction","Every Situation Eventually Reaches Tension Reduction","Energy decreases and rationality returns: the reachable, teachable moment.","Goal: re-establish therapeutic rapport.","Assess what the triggers were, whether coping strategies were used, and why they didn't work.","Help them identify coping strategies for the future.","Revise the plan of care to include the behaviors that may result in repeat seclusion or restraint."]},{"s":"Seclusion & Restraint","a":"seclusion-restraint","k":["Seclusion & Restraint","Seclusion and restraint are always a last resort. Try everything else first: verbal de-escalation, then pharmacologic interventions. Seclusion is typically used before restraints.","Seclusion — Involuntary confinement of a patient alone in a room or area they are physically prevented from leaving; may be a quiet area on the unit. Less restrictive than restraints and never punitive: the goal is safety. Only for violent …","Restraint — Any manual method, physical or mechanical device, material, or equipment that immobilizes or reduces a patient's ability to move arms, legs, body, or head freely. Risks staff injury and physical or emotional harm to the patient.","90–95% of patients in the psychiatric setting have a history of trauma or abuse, so holding someone down or mechanically restraining them can be traumatizing or re-traumatizing.","When It May Be Used: All Must Be True","As a last resort","Patient is a danger to self or others","Less restrictive interventions determined ineffective to protect the patient or others from harm","With a written modification to the plan of care","Only by safe, appropriate techniques per hospital policy and state law","What Counts","Physically holding a patient against their will is a restraint.","Four side rails up is a restraint, except for seizure precautions.","Tucking sheets so tightly the patient cannot move is a restraint.","Keeping a patient in their room by physical intervention is seclusion.","Guidelines for the Use of Mechanical Restraints","Mandated by the Joint Commission.","Indications for use — To protect the patient and others from harm.","Legal requirements — Multidisciplinary involvement · provider order per state law, within one hour of initiation, with a face-to-face assessment by someone external to the situation · patient advocate or relative notified · discontinued as …","Clinical assessment — Mental state at the time · physical examination for medical problems possibly causing the behavior change · the need for restraint.","Documentation — Behaviors leading to it · least restrictive measures tried first · interventions used and the response · the plan of care implemented · ongoing nursing evaluation.","Release procedure — Patient able to follow instructions and stay in control · termination of restraints · debrief with the patient.","Never restrain a patient in the prone position.","Types of Restraints","Nonviolent (soft) — The blue soft restraints. Monitoring and documentation frequency is lower.","Violent — Secure cuffs or leather restraints, much sturdier. Monitoring and documentation frequency is much higher.","The wrap — Like a security blanket; very effective for calming children and adolescents, usable for adults too.","Chairs and boards — Many facilities have moved to restraint chairs for children as safer than the restraint bed.","Monitoring a Patient in Restraints","The specific monitoring intervals are not exam material.","A patient in restraints is never left alone. Someone sits outside or inside the room with the door open, and the patient is directly observed and formally assessed at frequent intervals.","Level of awareness — Do they know what is going on, and where they are?","Level of activity — Thrashing in the restraints, or calm?","Vital signs — Part of the ongoing assessment.","Range of motion — Remove the restraints and do range of motion on all limbs, one at a time.","Circulation — Two fingers between the restraint and the limb; check capillary refill.","Hydration, nutrition, toileting — Fluids, food, and the opportunity for elimination.","Safety and comfort — Within the restraints.","A chaotic restraint event is extremely dangerous. Act as a team with a designated team leader who tells people what to do.","Organizing a Restraint Event","Assign every role first: who leads · who talks to the patient · who checks the seclusion area · who clears the path and moves other patients out · who prepares and gives the medication · who does the 1:1 in seclusion · who documents · who l…","Always place a wedge pillow under the head of anyone restrained in a bed; lying flat makes breathing harder, especially with a history of breathing difficulty. An extra-elevated wedge pillow exists for patients with COPD.","Restraining a child is more dangerous: the trachea, intercostal muscles, and diaphragm are underdeveloped and more pliable, so a restraint device restricts them more easily. Nonviolent Crisis Intervention (CPI) teaches holding techniques fo…","Contraindications for Seclusion & Restraint","The Six Contraindications","Extremely unstable medical and psychiatric conditions","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Spinal injury","Seizure disorders","Pregnancy","Delirium or dementia: seclusion may be intolerable because of the absence of stimulation"]},{"s":"Critical Incident Debriefing","a":"critical-incident-debriefing","k":["Critical Incident Debriefing","When It Happens","Immediate and mandatory after every restraint episode, with the patient and with staff who took part in or witnessed it.","If it cannot happen immediately, it must occur within the first couple of hours, or at least within the shift.","What It Addresses","Could anything have been done that would have prevented the episode? If yes, what, and why was it not done in this situation?","Did the team respond as a team?","Was safety maintained?","Did we follow hospital policies?","What could we have done differently, and what lessons were learned?","Areas of staff education that would help in future restraint episodes."]},{"s":"Teaching & Health Promotion","a":"teaching-health-promotion","k":["Teaching & Health Promotion","Role Model and Educator","Model appropriate responses and ways to cope with anger","Teach a variety of methods to appropriately express anger","Educate about coping skills, de-escalation techniques, and self-soothing skills to manage behavior","Assist in identifying triggers for anger and aggression","Above all, be proactive and respond early"]}]},{"f":"week1-therapies.html","t":"Assessment, Theories & Therapies","secs":[{"s":"Assessment, Theories & Therapies","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4"]},{"s":"The Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing Assessment","a":"the-psychiatric-mental-health-nursing-assessment","k":["The Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing Assessment","Do a complete assessment on every patient, in any practice setting, and understand the role the mind-body connection plays in mental health disorders.","Purpose of the Assessment","Establish rapport: the foundation of everything that follows.","Understand the current problem, including the chief complaint.","Review physical status: obtain baseline vital signs.","Assess risk and safety: risk factors, and anything affecting the safety of the patient and others on the unit.","Assess psychosocial status: the dimensions of the person's life.","Identify mutual goals: set treatment goals with the client.","Formulate a plan of care: document the data in a retrievable format.","Gathering the Data","Review of systems: vital signs and all systems.","Labs: bring abnormal results to the NP, PA, or MD.","Mental status: current cognitive processes, including behavior, speech, appearance, and mood.","Validation: previous records on the unit, police reports, emergency department records.","Rating scales: the PHQ-9 and the suicide risk screening.","Psychosocial assessment: occupation, sexuality, hobbies, spirituality, coping, relationships, lifestyle."]},{"s":"Analyzing & Recognizing Cues","a":"analyzing-recognizing-cues","k":["Analyzing & Recognizing Cues","What You Assess, and What You Are Looking For","Affect: what you see.","Thought processes and content: circumstantial or loose? Delusional? Any hallucinations? Any suicidal thoughts?","Judgment and insight: assessed as its own area.","Mood and feelings: in the person's own words.","Physical behavior and activity: eye contact, posture, agitation, psychomotor retardation.","Movement: tics, tremors, akathisia.","Communication: speech rate (rapid, slow, pressured), volume, and fluency.","If a person is depressed, they must be assessed for suicidal tendencies. Be aware of symptoms that can mask depression. If a patient seems depressed or down, do not be afraid to ask directly: \"Are you depressed?\" and, if yes, \"Do you feel t…","Affect","Affect is the observed responsiveness of a person's emotional state: the physical manifestations and observable behavior. Gauged two ways: range and congruency.","Range","Full / broad: normal expression of feeling, sad or happy; what most people without mental illness show.","Constricted: a little less expression than normal.","Blunted: limited expression, but some is still there; may smile a little at times.","Flat: no emotion on the face whatsoever, like the masking seen in Parkinson's. A blank canvas.","Congruency","Congruent: mood and affect match.","Incongruent: \"I'm extremely happy today,\" said flatly; crying while saying \"I'm doing great.\"","Sad also describes affect: eye contact downcast, stooped posture, and the facial expression.","Mood","Document the patient's own description of their mood; a psychiatric term can be added alongside it.","Euthymic — Normal mood.","Elated / euphoric — Abnormally elevated mood.","Dysthymic — Chronically low mood.","Labile — Mood goes up and down.","Cyclothymic — Cycling mood.","Everyday descriptors — Calm, anxious, agitated, overwhelmed, helpless, and hopeless, which is very important to document.","Screening Questions for Mood","\"How are you feeling?\"","\"Have you been discouraged / depressed / low / blue lately?\"","\"Have you been energized / elated / high / out of control lately?\""]},{"s":"Psychosocial Interventions","a":"psychosocial-interventions","k":["Psychosocial Interventions","Psychosocial interventions are non-pharmacological therapeutic techniques that address the psychological aspects (societal, familial, and cultural) through individual or group therapy, and promote mental wellness.","Group or individual therapy — Delivered one-to-one or in a group.","Music therapy — Strongly evidence-based, and not just psychiatric; now used in NICUs and other ICUs.","Education — On the illness, its symptoms, or its treatment.","Positive self-talk — Cognitive behavioral technique to modify negative thought patterns; improves self-esteem, resilience, and emotional regulation.","Self-monitoring — Journaling mood, feelings, or sleep; strong because the client can keep doing it after discharge.","Enhancing communication skills — Building healthy relationships, navigating social situations, expressing needs and emotions.","Healthy relationships — Reduce isolation; provide emotional and practical support.","Play therapy — For younger children.","Family consultations and sessions — Help relatives understand what is going on, help parents develop more effective parenting skills, and teach suicide risk and how to monitor for it. Also used where a client has a guardian, or a spouse.","Limit setting — For sexual preoccupation or agitation. Be matter-of-fact: \"What you're saying is inappropriate, and I will come back when we can interact better.\" Plan your response in advance.","Increasing coping skills and self-esteem — Recreational activities and therapies: a craft can become a coping skill and boost self-esteem. Group exercise: list three positive things about yourself.","Stress reduction and relaxation — Taught as strategies the client can use.","Spiritual support — Not necessarily about religion, but a sense of connection to something bigger than ourselves, including nature or art. Cognitive, experiential, and behavioral; gives a person strength.","Psychological safety — Everyone contributes in groups; no answer is wrong and there are no wrong questions.","Unconditional positive regard — Given to the client regardless.","Recognition of relapse symptoms — Nurses watch for them, and clients are taught to recognize their own.","Discharge planning — Counts as a psychosocial intervention in its own right.","Group therapy (the types, the therapeutic factors, and the roles) is taught in Group & Physiologic Therapies"]},{"s":"Psychoanalytic Theory & Therapy","a":"psychoanalytic-theory-therapy","k":["Psychoanalytic Theory & Therapy","Freud's theory, rarely used now in the acute setting. Freud believed mental disorders resulted from unresolved issues from childhood.","Three Levels of Awareness","Consciousness: everything one is aware of at a given time.","Preconsciousness: easily retrievable.","Unconscious: memories and emotions retrievable only with the help of a trained therapist. This is where a lot of clients' traumatic memories live.","Personality Structures","Id: present at birth; unconscious, impulsive, illogical.","Ego: begins around age 2–3; the problem-solver and reality-tester.","Superego: the moral part of personality.","Freud thought all three should work together, but that the ego is in charge.","Free association — The patient shares whatever is on their mind.","Dream analysis — Analyzing dream content for specific meanings; dreaming of falling may mean being unable to control situations.","Defense mechanism recognition — Defense mechanisms decrease anxiety and internal stress; help patients recognize and change the overuse of mechanisms like denial and rationalization. More in Chapter 15.","Purpose: uncovering unconscious conflicts. Not seen inpatient; it runs two to three times a week, for several years. What carries forward: childhood experience shapes personality development, and traumatic memories are unconscious."]},{"s":"Behavioral Theories & the Five Behavioral Therapies","a":"behavioral-theories-the-five-behavioral-therapies","k":["Behavioral Theories & the Five Behavioral Therapies","Personality consists of learned behavior, influenced by conditioning: pairing a behavior with a condition that reinforces or decreases it (Pavlov's dog and the bell). Three textbook theories gave rise to behavioral therapy: classical condit…","Modeling — Demonstrating behaviors more effective than the current ones; a lot like role-playing.","Operant conditioning — Using positive reinforcement to increase the behaviors you want to see: tokens earned for going to groups, completing ADLs, and asking for meds, rising through levels that unlock privileges.","Systematic desensitization — Evidence-based; combines relaxation techniques with gradual exposure to overcome a phobia. Can be virtual, in vivo (real world), or imaginal.","Aversion therapy — The opposite of operant conditioning: a negative stimulus to decrease unwanted behavior. Disulfiram (Antabuse) in alcohol dependence: drinking on it causes nausea, dizziness, and flushing, so drinking decreases.","Biofeedback — The patient learns to control normally involuntary bodily responses (blood pressure, brain waves, heart rate) using electrical sensors that feed back information about the body. Used for stress, pain, anxiety, headaches, and A…","Modeling = role playing · operant conditioning = tokens / positive reinforcement · systematic desensitization = exposure · aversion therapy = negative stimulus decreases behavior · biofeedback = control of body."]},{"s":"Talk Therapies & Interpersonal Therapy","a":"talk-therapies-interpersonal-therapy","k":["Talk Therapies & Interpersonal Therapy","Medications and talk therapy are the two main treatments for mental illness.","Interpersonal therapy targets relationships by improving functioning and communication patterns. Very short-term: 12 to 16 sessions.","Identification of emotion — Identify what the emotion is and where it is coming from.","Expression of emotion — Express it in a healthy way: calmly telling a partner how you feel, instead of acting out of anger and sarcasm.","Dealing with emotional baggage — Unresolved issues from past relationships are carried into present ones; examining them shows how they affect current mood and behavior."]},{"s":"Cognitive Therapy & CBT","a":"cognitive-therapy-cbt","k":["Cognitive Therapy & CBT","Cognitive therapy is the umbrella term for therapies focused on thoughts (cognitions), with a dynamic interplay between the individual and the environment.","The core CBT idea: our thoughts cause our feelings and behaviors, not external things like people or situations. So if we change the way we think, we can change the way we feel, and act better even if the situation does not change.","Cognitive Distortions: \"Stinkin' Thinkin'\"","Part of CBT: taking the irrational internal commentary we run all day and making it more rational and more positive. Developed at Hazelden, then taken further by David Burns in The Feeling Good Handbook; the set is his \"Top 10 Stinkin' Thin…","1. All-or-nothing thinking — Seeing things in black or white.","2. Overgeneralization — A single negative event becomes always or never: \"I'm never going to get a promotion.\"","3. Mental filter — Dwelling excessively on one negative and ignoring all the positive feedback.","4. Discounting the positive — Rejecting positive experiences: \"that doesn't count,\" \"that was just a fluke.\"","5. Jumping to conclusions — Interpreting things negatively with no facts: mind reading and fortune telling.","6. Magnification — Blowing something out of proportion.","7. Emotional reasoning — Treating a feeling as evidence of fact.","8. Should statements — \"I should have done this, I should have done that.\"","9. Labeling — All-or-nothing thinking taken to an extreme: instead of \"I made a mistake,\" you say \"I'm a loser.\"","10. Personalization and blame — Holding yourself personally responsible for something that was not even under your control."]},{"s":"Rational Emotive Therapy (RET)","a":"rational-emotive-therapy-ret","k":["Rational Emotive Therapy (RET)","RET explores irrational thinking and beliefs: identifying activating situations and the negative emotions that lead to irrational beliefs, then teaching the client to delete the \"must,\" \"should,\" \"ought,\" and \"have to.\"","The ABCs of RET","Activating event: \"I receive an invitation to a party.\"","Beliefs: \"I hate parties, I don't like hanging out with people.\"","Consequences, emotional: \"I feel miserable.\"","The therapy works on B, because changing beliefs changes the emotional consequences.","Desired outcome: the client controls their behavior and thinking, and changed thinking produces changed behavior."]},{"s":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)","a":"dialectical-behavior-therapy-dbt","k":["Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)","DBT involves the integration of opposites. Originally developed to treat chronically suicidal individuals, many of whom had borderline personality disorder. It also helps with other self-destructive or self-injurious behaviors such as eatin…","What it combines — Cognitive and behavioral techniques combined with mindfulness.","Setting — Both inpatient and outpatient, but more often outpatient, because it takes one to two years before you see the best results.","Core interventions — Problem-solving · exposure technique · skill training · contingency management · cognitive modification.","The four primary modes of treatment — Group skills training · individual psychotherapy · telephone contact · therapist consultation and team meetings.","The team — Several people, including an MD psychiatrist, not just a therapist or social worker. You have one primary therapist you check in with regularly.","The Four Skill Sets","Emotional regulation — Managing and labeling intense moods and emotions.","Interpersonal effectiveness — Problem-solving skills and assertiveness, including being able to say no, which many people find very hard.","Distress tolerance — Also appears as self-management: \"learning to control, manage, or change your behaviors, thoughts, and emotions.\"","Mindfulness — Being present in the moment; meditation skills.","Mindfulness: indulging a wandering mind reinforces the emotions that make us suffer, and most negative thoughts are about the past or the future. The past is over; the future is unreal until it happens; the present moment is the only one yo…"]}]},{"f":"week1-group-physiologic-therapies.html","t":"Group & Physiologic Therapies","secs":[{"s":"Group & Physiologic Therapies","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topics 5 & 6"]},{"s":"Group Dynamics","a":"group-dynamics","k":["Group Dynamics","Group work: individuals with a common purpose come together and benefit by mutually giving and receiving feedback. A group is two or more people pursuing common goals or interests, so patient and family education counts as leading a group.","Characteristics of a Group","Size","Defined purpose","Degree of similarity: members share the problem the group is built around, even when the similarity isn't visible.","Rules: one person speaks at a time; no phone calls.","Boundaries","Content and process: what is talked about, and what goes on inside the group.","Who Can Lead What","A registered nurse: activity, educational, task, and support groups.","Advanced degree required: group psychotherapy, aimed at bringing about personality change."]},{"s":"Types of Groups","a":"types-of-groups","k":["Types of Groups","Psychoeducational groups increase knowledge about a specific psychological or somatic subject. The group types and Yalom's factors are also the categories the peer support paper has to cover.","Medication or health education — Symptom management or disease education, such as a diabetes group on a med-surg floor.","Dual diagnosis — Both a substance use disorder and another mental health disorder, such as major depressive disorder.","Symptom management — Managing anxiety; stress management.","Recreational activity — Ping pong, the art room, cards. Not recreational therapy, which requires a recreational therapist or someone with an advanced degree.","Interpersonal skills — Enhances knowledge and improves skills so clients can solve their problems.","Therapeutic Community Meeting","Essential group on the inpatient unit, usually in the morning.","Covers disruptions and disagreements on the unit and reinforces the rules: ten minutes at a time on the phone, because others need it.","Where the unit works out how to make things better.","Support / Self-Help Groups","Very structured.","Provide the opportunity to maintain or enhance personal and social functioning through cooperation and shared understanding of life's challenges.","AA, cancer survivors, bereavement, substance abuse, surviving suicide. Can be very empowering.","The therapeutic milieu and its five components are taught in Introduction to Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing"]},{"s":"Yalom's Therapeutic Factors","a":"yalom-s-therapeutic-factors","k":["Yalom's Therapeutic Factors","Yalom identified 11 factors that make group therapy work.","Instillation of hope — Leader and members share optimism about improvement: \"you got better, so maybe I can too.\"","Universality — Clients realize they are not alone: \"I'm not the only one who hears voices,\" \"I'm not the only one with thoughts of suicide.\"","Imparting of information — Participants receive formal teaching from the leader, or advice from peers.","Altruism — Members gain from giving support to others, which improves self-value; doing it for someone else, not for yourself.","Corrective recapitulation — Members repeat behavior patterns learned in their families, get feedback, and work out how to correct the unhealthy ones.","Development of socializing techniques — New social skills learned from feedback and modeling; the group is a safe place to try them.","Imitative behavior — Members copy behavior from leaders or peers and adopt healthier habits.","Interpersonal learning — Insight into themselves from others' feedback, with the choice to change. Happens in later phases, in an established group.","Group cohesiveness — In a mature group, each member feels connected to the others, the leader, and the group as a whole, and can accept both positive feedback and constructive criticism.","Catharsis — A therapeutic discharge of emotion through experiencing and expressing feelings: \"this has really helped me get in touch with my sadness.\"","Existential resolution — Members examine what affects everyone (loneliness, mortality, responsibility) and construct meaning."]},{"s":"Advantages & Disadvantages of Group Therapy","a":"advantages-disadvantages-of-group-therapy","k":["Advantages & Disadvantages of Group Therapy","Advantages","Engages multiple clients in treatment at the same time, which is why group is the main treatment on the units.","Participants benefit from the feedback, knowledge, and life experience of others.","A safe setting to try out new behaviors and communication skills.","Promotes a feeling of belonging.","Disadvantages","Time constraints: a unit group runs about 45 minutes; with 15 clients, participation time feels short, especially if someone monopolizes it.","Confidentiality cannot be guaranteed: no promise that every member keeps what is said in the group.","Disruptive members. Someone acutely psychotic, manic, or intoxicated cannot sit through a group or interact effectively and does not benefit from group treatment; leaders will try to get them out of it."]},{"s":"Group Leadership & Member Roles","a":"group-leadership-member-roles","k":["Group Leadership & Member Roles","Leadership Styles","Autocratic — Exerts control, runs the group, and does not encourage much interaction between members, as in an educational group heavy on information.","Democratic — Supports extensive group interaction in the process of problem-solving: psychotherapy groups, and AA.","Laissez-faire — Allows members to behave however they choose and does not control the group's direction; starts it and lets it go where it goes. AA groups are usually democratic or laissez-faire.","Task Roles","Initiator-contributor — Suggests new ideas, or different ways of seeing the problem or goal.","Information seeker — Tries to clarify the group's goals.","Information giver — Provides facts and shares experiences as an authority figure.","Coordinator — Clarifies how different ideas can work together.","Orienteer — Notes the group's progress toward its goals.","Recorder — Keeps notes and acts as the group's memory.","Building & Maintenance Roles","Oriented toward the group's function: they strengthen, regulate, and keep it going.","Encourager — Praises, and seeks input.","Harmonizer — Reconciles differences among members.","Gatekeeper — Facilitates the contributions of others, to keep communication open.","Group observer — Notes and reports what is occurring in the group process.","Follower — Goes along with others, an audience role.","Individual Roles","Meet the need of only one member and hamper rather than enhance group functioning.","Aggressor — Criticizes or attacks others' ideas or feelings.","Blocker — Oppositional: resists progress; argues or disagrees beyond reason.","Recognition seeker — Calls attention to themselves; boasts about their achievements.","Play person — Horses around, plays on their phone, tells jokes.","Dominator — Asserts authority and superiority; manipulates or interrupts others, sometimes with flattery.","Handling Challenging Members","Monopolizing member — Address it as the group goes: everyone has ten minutes, or an equal chance to contribute. Sometimes take the member aside privately after the meeting.","Demoralizing member — Listen objectively, then ask in private what is going on with their anger. Be matter-of-fact.","Silent member — Determine the cause. Provide extra time to think about a response and come back to them.","The member who needs extra time — Slowed psychomotor functioning in severe depression means a longer wait for an answer. Silence is not always resistance; offer to come back to them at the end of group."]},{"s":"Phototherapy (Light Therapy)","a":"phototherapy-light-therapy","k":["Phototherapy (Light Therapy)","The device — A fluorescent light box with a strong UV filter, about a 10,000 lux light source.","How it is used — Set it off to the side: do not look directly into it. 30 to 45 minutes a day, two to three times a week, usually in the morning, most often during fall and winter months.","Indications — Depression occurring during pregnancy, so medications can be avoided; and major depressive disorder with a seasonal pattern, where it is first-line treatment.","Terminology change — Seasonal affective disorder (SAD) was removed in DSM-5 and reclassified as major depressive disorder with seasonal onset / seasonal pattern.","Mechanism — Efficacy is credited to the influence of light on melatonin; it stimulates the pineal gland, increasing melatonin and serotonin.","How well it works — As effective as medication for SAD.","Side effects — Headache and jitteriness; also eye strain, and rarely nausea, sweating, or sedation. Most people have none at all.","Light therapy can trigger mania or hypomania. Before prescribing it for depression, investigate thoroughly to make sure the diagnosis is not actually bipolar disorder. Otherwise it is very safe."]},{"s":"Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT)","a":"electroconvulsive-therapy-ect","k":["Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT)","ECT is the most effective treatment for depression, and it is non-invasive.","Indications","Severe long-term depression: primary treatment with malnutrition, exhaustion, or dehydration. Rehydrate before ECT.","Certain medical conditions: safer than medications in some conditions; avoids polypharmacy in the elderly.","Delusional depression: works very well.","Psychotic illnesses: the second most common indication for ECT.","Refractory depression: has not responded to multiple medication trials, or keeps recurring despite treatment. Not usually first-line; ECT follows failed trials.","Catatonia: works especially well in schizophrenia with catatonia.","The Procedure","Anesthesia: done under anesthesia with a muscle relaxant.","Electrode placement: electricity applied to the temporal regions — unilateral (one side) or bilateral (an electrode on each temple).","Course: two to three treatments a week, for a total of 6 to 12 treatments.","The stimulus: an electrical current lasting 1 second or less.","The seizure: a brief seizure of 30 to 60 seconds, monitored throughout. One extremity is left un-paralyzed so the convulsion can be observed.","With or without medication: can be used either way; sometimes it helps boost SSRIs. Improves energy, appetite, sleep, and mood.","How it works: not exactly known. Theories: alters neurotransmitter receptors and can increase serotonin; regulates stress hormones; increases cerebral blood flow, possibly neurogenesis.","Cardiac: the heart is heavily stressed at the onset of the seizure and for up to ten minutes after; assess and manage in hypertension, congestive heart failure, cardiac arrhythmias, and other cardiac conditions.Neurologic: the brain is stre…","Waking — About 15 minutes after the procedure.","Confusion and disorientation — Very common, lasting several hours. Reorienting afterward is part of the procedure, not just a reaction to it; tell the family of an outpatient they may need to do the same.","Retrograde amnesia — Loss of the events leading up to and including the treatment itself, usually just the morning before and shortly after. Most patients experience it."]},{"s":"TMS, Vagus Nerve Stimulation & Deep Brain Stimulation","a":"tms-vagus-nerve-stimulation-deep-brain-stimulation","k":["TMS, Vagus Nerve Stimulation & Deep Brain Stimulation","Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (TMS): Non-Invasive","What it is — MRI-strength magnetic pulses that stimulate focal areas of the cerebral cortex. FDA approved for treatment-resistant depression.","The course — Outpatient, 30 minutes, five days a week for four to six weeks.","What happens — Patient is awake and alert. An electromagnet on the scalp sends short pulses through the prefrontal cortex, inducing neurons to fire. Feels like a slight tapping, sometimes with scalp contractions and jaw tightening.","Safety profile — No neurological deficits, no memory problems, no loss of consciousness; seizures rarely. Most reactions mild: scalp tingling and discomfort at the site. Patients can continue their medications.","Adverse reactions — Headache and lightheadedness.","The one contraindication for TMS is metal in the area of stimulation: brain stimulators, cochlear implants, medication pumps, bullet fragments. Also not used in anyone with a pacemaker or a history of seizures.","Vagus Nerve Stimulation (VNS): Invasive","Origin — Originally for epilepsy; it decreases seizures and improves mood, so it is FDA approved for partial onset seizures and depression.","The device — A pulse generator the size of a pacemaker in the chest, wired up and wrapped around the vagus nerve in the neck.","How it fires — Stimulates the left cervical vagus nerve, pulsing 30 seconds every 5 minutes, 24 hours a day.","Mechanism — Boosts neurotransmitter levels by activating the limbic system.","Used for — Invasive, so generally reserved for treatment-resistant depression.","Side effects — Voice alteration in nearly 60% of patients, plus hoarseness, headache, neck pain, cough, paresthesia, and dyspnea, all decreasing with time. Implanting a device also carries infection risk.","Deep Brain Stimulation (DBS): Most Invasive","Very seldom used. Electrodes implanted in the chest, threaded up the neck and into the scalp, with probes placed into the brain.","A treatment for Parkinson's disease, now investigated in depression, stimulating regions identified as underactive in depression.","Risk is not only chest infection; something is placed inside the brain."]},{"s":"Exercise & Herbal Treatments","a":"exercise-herbal-treatments","k":["Exercise & Herbal Treatments","Exercise","Combats the harmful effects of stress, anxiety, and depression; any exercise is good.","Regular exercisers with depression and anxiety report elevated mood and greater happiness.","Alters serotonin, dopamine, and norepinephrine, and dampens the HPA axis, thought to be overly active in depression.","Has biological, social, and psychological effects.","Less expensive than ECT, fewer side effects than medication.","Herbal treatments are one of the seven key terms to know.","St. John's Wort — Mild-to-moderate depression. A flower processed into tea or tablets, thought to increase serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine.","Ginseng — Stress or fatigue.","Valerian root · Chamomile — Depression, insomnia, stress, or anxiety.","SAM-e — Depression.","Herbals are not regulated by the FDA. The concentration of active ingredients varies between brands and formulas, and much more research is needed."]}]}];
